<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <!--
    The compiler switches every project in this tree shares (ADR-018 A24,
    review finding CPP-4).

    Two of these four were never written down anywhere. /fp:precise and the
    absence of /arch are what ADR-005 §6's same-binary determinism actually
    rests on, and both were MSVC defaults that the design documents described in
    prose as if they had been chosen. A default is not a decision: nothing
    stopped the next contributor, or the next property page, from changing one,
    and the symptom would have been replay hashes parting with no line to blame.

    Imported from each project's PropertySheets ImportGroup, which is the one
    place a shared sheet reliably beats the toolset's defaults and still loses
    to a project that deliberately overrides it. No project overrides these: the
    CI step "Check the build invariants are centralised" fails the build on a
    .vcxproj that spells one of them, and a second step reads the switches back
    off the compiler command lines in build.log, so the invariant is checked
    where it actually takes effect rather than where it is written.
  -->
  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <!-- /std:c++latest and /permissive-, both AGENTS.md §5 rules. -->
      <LanguageStandard>stdcpplatest</LanguageStandard>
      <ConformanceMode>true</ConformanceMode>

      <!--
        /fp:precise, stated. /fp:fast licenses the compiler to reassociate float
        expressions, so two translation units of the same simulation built under
        it can disagree about the same sum. AGENTS.md §5 bans it in GameLogic;
        this is that ban made mechanical, and made solution-wide, because the
        client re-runs the same shared functions on the same quantised inputs.
      -->
      <FloatingPointModel>Precise</FloatingPointModel>

      <!--
        No /arch. x64's baseline is SSE2, and raising it (AVX, AVX2) changes
        which instructions the compiler picks for the same float expression, so
        a build with /arch and one without are two different binaries by
        ADR-005 §6's reckoning and their replay hashes part. NotSet emits no
        switch at all: the state this tree is in today, and now the state it is
        held in.
      -->
      <EnableEnhancedInstructionSet>NotSet</EnableEnhancedInstructionSet>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
</Project>
